      "message": "Well, we could, the chances of that are 1/(2^(pcr number of bits)), right?\n\nNote that ending up with PCR of all zeros is just as likely as ending up with PCR of all ones.\n\nConsidering these two conditions to be marks of an uniitialized PCR greatly simplifies the code, do you think this is a problem and we should not do it?",
      "parentUuid": "9233c29b_548db25b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 959,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 959,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "24f6c507_c37f772c",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 959,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-29T21:48:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, I agree with the odds, you are probably right. It just something in me that protests against treating 00..00 and FF..FF as the same value in case we start both values as some sort of an indicator in the future (which we likely never will). :)\n\nWe have 6 PCRs (17-22), for which initial value is FF..FF (and we never use them in practice, so they are always stay at FF..FF). How important is it to avoid storing them?\n\nBtw, what happens in this case, if we have, say, only PCRs 0 \u0026 1, which are neither 00 nor FF.\nUpon restoring the PCRs after save-state-and-reboot, will 17-22 go to FF as expected?",
      "parentUuid": "a63a8945_ffbe0bfa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 959,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 959,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "900dd61f_cffbf69f",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 959,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-30T00:43:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "so, as discussed, it turns out that those PCRs in question are not held in the NVMEM, so luckily there is nothing which needs to be done here :)",
      "parentUuid": "24f6c507_c37f772c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 959,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 959,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0f343152_af2221a4",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 959,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T20:14:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ok, but that\u0027s our current implementation. I\u0027m fine with landing as-is to avoid delaying the patch further, but can we add some ideally compile-time check that all PCRs that we save in nvmem have default values of 0..0?",
      "parentUuid": "900dd61f_cffbf69f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 959,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 959,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
