    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c80d13bd_c044f592",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2692,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-29T00:21:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If Cr50 dies here the init function will make sure that there is only one instance of var with this key is there, and we don\u0027t really care that this is the old one, because if we crashed, the process must have not completed anyways.",
      "parentUuid": "15260864_e96d503b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2692,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 2692,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "149c879f_028c266d",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2692,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-29T21:48:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why would init do that?\nwe have a new variable written and a proper delimiter after it (save_var() does add_final_delimiter() in case of success). it will just continue using nvmem as-is. and nvmem will have two copies of the var. we will continue reading the old one, which is ok. but if we later delete it or change it again, the 1st one will be deleted, but the 2nd one then will become \u0027active\u0027.",
      "parentUuid": "c80d13bd_c044f592",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2692,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 2692,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d70be28d_43db6b2b",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2692,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-30T00:43:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why would init make sure that there is only one instance of the variable? I think this is a reasonable thing to do. \n\nthere are three cases during initialization:\n  - there is a finalized delimiter at the top of the flash. This is the normal state, no further processing is required.\n\n   - there is a delimiter at the top, but it is not finalized. In this case all tuples must be OK, because the new tuple is saved only after the previous instance (if existed) was erased\n\n   - there is no delimiter at the end of the flash. This is an indication of the H1 crashed, or power yanked at the wrong time. The assumption made is that we should not worry too much/trust too to whatever was written after the last valid delimiter, and should just get rid of it all, because there is no way to verify what is what. This includes the tuples found above the last valid delimiter.\n\nWe might want to keep the tuples present after the last delimiter in this case, how about I open a tracker issue to discuss what we want to do and address it later.",
      "parentUuid": "149c879f_028c266d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2692,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 2692,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9345a482_6c864acd",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2692,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T20:14:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "here is how we change a tuple in setvar() in case the key already exists:\n1) call save_var() with generation++ for new value\n  a) in save_var(): save_container() adds a new tuple to the end of nvmem\n  b) in save_var(): add_final_delimiter() adds a new finalized delimiter to the end of nvmem\n2) call invalidate_object() for old tuple: sets its type to NN_OBJ_OLD_COPY\n\nIf the power is lost between 1b and 2, we have two tuples and a finalized delimiter.\nIf we reboot and attempt to read it, we\u0027ll get the old value, which is ok-ish since setvar technically hasn\u0027t completed yet.\nBut if later we call setvar(..., NULL, 0) to erase that variable, for example, it will find the 1st tuple and call invalidate_object() for it; so the the 2nd tuple will then become visible, and subsequent attempts to read that var will return it.\nSimilarly, a later setvar that changes the variable value again will do 1a and 1b and add a 3rd tuple, then erase the 1st tuple at step 2. And read attempts will return the value from tuple 2.",
      "parentUuid": "d70be28d_43db6b2b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2692,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 2692,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7a8994f7_9fe75725",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2692,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T22:41:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "ah, you are right, I already forgot that after writing vars we also adding the delimiter.\n\nI think the procedure should be changed such that non-finalized delimiter is added if this is a re-write of the var, and the delimiter should be finalized after the old copy is deleted. I doon\u0027t want to rush this fix now, is it OK if I add it to the new tracker issue?",
      "parentUuid": "9345a482_6c864acd",
