    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "41fe9b6d_f3a1cf47",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2751,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-30T00:43:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think adding another object type just for this case is a simplification: we will have to start checking for it everywhere we look at object types, does not seem very reasonable to me.\n\nNote that the last tuple does not have to fit into the page, in fact it is expected to spill over, I modified the code to limit the spillover to the minimum (and fixed a bug in value size calculations as a result).\n\nThe TPM wipe out test still passes.",
      "parentUuid": "ff15e889_33119d24",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2749,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 2751,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1b90054e_7404e745",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2751,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T20:14:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t agree that it\u0027s not a simplification, but fine. Calculating the required size of the container and adding a specialized container once sounds like a simpler and more reliable procedure than repurposing a meaningful container type and then adding it in the loop due to that chosen container type size limitations, where each \u0027add container\u0027 operation can fail (we are already recovering from some weird state, so this flash page is definitely unreliable).\nBtw, what happens if we write that variable once, but then fail to write again. We end up with a page with some tuple that contains some fake k,v pair. I don\u0027t worry too much about ending up in that state, but why even have a possibility of that? Recovery procedures should be as simple as possible. But that\u0027s just my opinion.",
      "parentUuid": "41fe9b6d_f3a1cf47",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2749,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 2751,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "961d2908006ae1ddeb4728590806a583c8640a8c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce1ada29_e3bbbd9e",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 2751,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-04-01T22:41:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I hear what you are saying, but still am very reluctant to allocate a new object type for this, the object type name space is very limited. \n\nMaybe we could save a TPM object with some well know index for this. Or maybe we can modify compaction to start on the next page to make sure that the current top page would be recycled.\n\nLet me add this to the list of open issues.",
      "parentUuid": "1b90054e_7404e745",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2749,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 2751,
        "endChar": 37
      },
